Local regression set — NOT part of the Cactus testsuite
======================================================

The Cactus testsuite driver (RunTest.pl) globs `test/*.par` only, so
nothing in this directory is run automatically.  These are the heavier
runs that the review criteria (under 10 s per test, under 2 min for the
whole component) do not allow in `test/`: `circ_orbit_amr` alone takes
about 20 minutes on one core.

They are kept because they cover things the small tests do not: 100-
particle rings, 3-level AMR with particles crossing level boundaries,
the TwoPunctures BBH sets, and the conv100 sampler comparisons at
1 / 2 / 4 ranks.

How to run them
---------------

`run_tests.sh` runs only the registered tests in `test/`; it does not run
this directory.  Run these configurations one at a time from a scratch
directory:

    mkdir /tmp/rt && cd /tmp/rt
    OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 mpirun -np 4 \
        <Cactus>/exe/cactus_sim \
        <Cactus>/arrangements/EinsteinAnalysis/Geodesic/test/extra/conv100_local27.par
    cmp conv100_local27/geodesic_particles.txt \
        <Cactus>/arrangements/EinsteinAnalysis/Geodesic/test/extra/conv100_local27/geodesic_particles.txt

`check_mpi_identity.sh` (in the parent directory) runs the 1-, 2- and
4-rank bit-identity comparison against `extra/conv100_local27.par` by
default, and against any other par you pass it.

Reference data
--------------

Each `<name>/geodesic_particles.txt` is the dump of a verified run of the
matching par.  The conv100 and tp_* references were regenerated on
2026-10-28 with the 10-column dump (revision 2026.10.28-1 added
`cctk_time`); the `*.regen.out` and `*.out` files in this directory are
the console logs of those runs, kept for reference only.

`circ_orbit_dense.par` is the exception: it has no reference dump because
it is not a regression test.  It is `circ_orbit.par` with
`Geodesic::step = 4` and `cctk_itlast = 800` instead of 100 and 32, so the
dump has 800 samples instead of 32.  The reason is a plotting one: 32
samples spread over 17.06 revolutions is 167.8 deg per frame, and joining
consecutive samples draws a {32/15} star polygon that looks like a rose.
The orbit is a circle either way -- `r` stays at 11.989 to within 2.7e-7
over t = 0..3200 -- and at 4 time units per dump the x-y plot is the
circle it already is numerically.  Verified 2026-11-08: 800 steps, 803
dump lines (800 data rows, t = 4..3200), `r` within 2.1e-7 of 11.989,
~90 s wall at 1 rank x 1 thread.  Since 2026-11-09 eight of the nine
Kerr testsuite tests take 37.5 samples per revolution themselves (9.6 deg
per frame) and plot as circles, so this par is only needed to draw the
dense curve next to the still-sparse `circ_orbit` dump.
